Thanks for reporting @pmourlanne. It looks like there's a strange behavior with extended_bounds combined with an offset.

I used the following scenario to reproduce on 5.2.2 and the last result does look strange to me too. @cbuescher worked on extended bounds, maybe you have an idea?

```

PUT /events/ '{
    "mappings" : {
      "event" : {
        "properties" : {
          "start_time" : {
            "type" : "date"
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }'

POST /events/event '{  
  "start_time": "2016-01-01T01:00:00.000Z"
}'

POST /events/event '{  
  "start_time": "2016-01-01T03:00:00.000Z"
}'

POST /events/event '{  
  "start_time": "2016-01-01T05:00:00.000Z"
}'
```
Aggregation with extended bounds and no offset returns:
<details>

```
POST '/_search?size=0' '{
    "aggs": {
        "start_time": {
            "date_histogram": {
                "extended_bounds": {
                    "min": "2016-01-01T06:00:00.000Z",
                    "max": "2016-01-03T08:00:00.000Z"
                },
                "field": "start_time",
                "interval": "1d",
                "min_doc_count": 0
            }
        }
    }
}'
{
  ... 
  "aggregations" : {
    "start_time" : {
      "buckets" : [
        {
          "key_as_string" : "2016-01-01T00:00:00.000Z",
          "key" : 1451606400000,
          "doc_count" : 3
        },
        {
          "key_as_string" : "2016-01-02T00:00:00.000Z",
          "key" : 1451692800000,
          "doc_count" : 0
        },
        {
          "key_as_string" : "2016-01-03T00:00:00.000Z",
          "key" : 1451779200000,
          "doc_count" : 0
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
```
</details>

And the same with offset `+1h` returns a strange result:
<details>

```
POST '/_search?size=0' '{
    "aggs": {
        "start_time": {
            "date_histogram": {
                "extended_bounds": {
                    "min": "2016-01-01T06:00:00.000Z",
                    "max": "2016-01-03T08:00:00.000Z"
                },
                "field": "start_time",
                "interval": "1d",
                "min_doc_count": 0,
                "offset": "+1h"
            }
        }
    }
}'
{
  ...
  "aggregations" : {
    "start_time" : {
      "buckets" : [
        {
          "key_as_string" : "2016-01-01T00:00:00.000Z",
          "key" : 1451606400000,
          "doc_count" : 0
        },
        {
          "key_as_string" : "2016-01-01T01:00:00.000Z",
          "key" : 1451610000000,
          "doc_count" : 3
        },
        {
          "key_as_string" : "2016-01-02T00:00:00.000Z",
          "key" : 1451692800000,
          "doc_count" : 0
        },
        {
          "key_as_string" : "2016-01-03T00:00:00.000Z",
          "key" : 1451779200000,
          "doc_count" : 0
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
```
</details>

issue.body:
Working on #23789 we came upon some issue around `extended_bounds` in `date_histogram` when some rounding is used. The behaviour seems to be a bit unclear, so thats what this issue is about.

Currently, when using `interval` rounding of e.g. a day and providing extended bounds that are not rounded values, we round them down, e.g.:

```
GET index/test/_search
{
  "size": 0,
  "aggs": {
    "my_agg": {
      "date_histogram": {
        "extended_bounds": {
          "min": "2017-04-10T06:00:00Z",
          "max": "2017-04-13T08:00:00Z"
        },
        "field": "date",
        "interval": "1d",
        "min_doc_count": 0
      }
    }
  }
}
```
```
"aggregations": {
    "my_agg": {
      "buckets": [
        {
          "key_as_string": "2017-04-10T00:00:00.000Z",
          "key": 1491782400000,
          "doc_count": 0
        },
        {
          "key_as_string": "2017-04-11T00:00:00.000Z",
          "key": 1491868800000,
          "doc_count": 0
        },
        {
          "key_as_string": "2017-04-12T00:00:00.000Z",
          "key": 1491955200000,
          "doc_count": 1
        },
        {
          "key_as_string": "2017-04-13T00:00:00.000Z",
          "key": 1492041600000,
          "doc_count": 0
        }
      ]
    }
  }
```

To me that seems like a good behaviour because it leaves one edge buckets that overlaps the specified extended bound date. I furthermore think that any additional `offset` should shift the the buckets after rounding by a fixed amount (see https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/issues/23776 for a current bug with this)
